> =====
> planner/plans.py
> """
> Plan specification, templating, and instantiation.
> 
> This module defines the structure of a Plan, how PlanSpecs are templated from
> registered models and tools, and how they are instantiated into concrete
> candidate plans ready for selection and execution.
> 
> Important design rules:
> - Plans are immutable after instantiation.
> - Plan identity incorporates model/tool versions, prompts/instructions,
>   responder type, risk policy, and configuration variants that materially
>   affect behavior.
> - Tool configuration is a first-class decision variable.
> - Avoid arbitrary combinatorial model-tool generation. Start with a small,
>   explicit, bounded family of plans.
> - Examples: direct response; model + calculator; model + memory retrieval;
>   model + web/source grounding; draft + independent reviewer.
> 
> Expected helpers:
> 
> class PlanTemplate(Protocol):
>     def instantiate_plan(
>         self,
>         template_id: str,
>         model_specs: Mapping[str, ModelSpec],
>         tool_specs: Mapping[str, ToolSpec],
>         context: PlannerContext,
>         **template_args: Any,
>     ) -> PlanSpec: ...
> 
> def create_direct_plan_template(...) -> PlanTemplate: ...
> def create_tool_use_plan_template(...) -> PlanTemplate: ...
> def create_rag_plan_template(...) -> PlanTemplate: ...
> def create_reviewed_plan_template(...) -> PlanTemplate: ...
> 
> def generate_plans_from_templates(
>     templates: Sequence[PlanTemplate],
>     context: PlannerContext,
>     registry: Registry,
> ) -> list[PlanSpec]:
>     '''
>     Instantiate only plans feasible and eligible for this context. For example,
>     exclude plans whose required model/tool is unavailable, disallowed for the
>     risk category, or incompatible with available token budget.
>     '''
>     ...
> 
> Caller:
> - planner.py asks this module for current candidate plans.
> - registry.py stores explicitly registered plans.
> - selection.py receives concrete PlanSpecs, never templates.
> - responders.py interprets the selected plan's responder/template metadata.
> """
> 
